`),choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["The iron is a catalyst. A catalyst does not change the energies of the reactants or the products; it offers a different route between them whose energy barrier (the transition state) is lower.","","On an energy profile, the forward activation energy is measured from the reactants up to the top of the barrier, and the reverse activation energy from the products up to the same top. Lowering the top of the barrier therefore reduces **both** values by exactly the same amount, and $\\Delta H$ (the gap between reactants and products) is unchanged.","","Why the other options fail:","- A catalyst cannot help only one direction. If it did, it would shift the equilibrium, which catalysts never do: they only let equilibrium be reached sooner.","- Raising the reverse barrier while lowering the forward one would change $\\Delta H$, which a catalyst cannot do.","- A catalyst does not work by increasing the collision rate; its effect comes from the lower-energy pathway, so a larger fraction of collisions have enough energy to react."].join(`
