`),sampleAnswer:["The mixture is a buffer: it contains a weak base, $\\mathrm{NH_3}$, and its conjugate acid, $\\mathrm{NH_4^+}$, both in significant amounts. They are linked by this equilibrium:","","$$\\mathrm{NH_3(aq) + H_2O(l) \\rightleftharpoons NH_4^+(aq) + OH^-(aq)}$$","","**Adding nitric acid.** The added $\\mathrm{H_3O^+}$ ions react with $\\mathrm{OH^-}$, and the large reserve of $\\mathrm{NH_3}$ also takes them up directly:","","$$\\mathrm{NH_3(aq) + H_3O^+(aq) \\rightarrow NH_4^+(aq) + H_2O(l)}$$","","Removing $\\mathrm{OH^-}$ makes the equilibrium shift to the right (Le Chatelier's principle), producing more $\\mathrm{OH^-}$ from $\\mathrm{NH_3}$. The added acid is converted into the weak acid $\\mathrm{NH_4^+}$, so $[\\mathrm{OH^-}]$, and hence the pH, hardly changes.","","**Adding potassium hydroxide.** The extra $\\mathrm{OH^-}$ is consumed by the reserve of $\\mathrm{NH_4^+}$:","","$$\\mathrm{NH_4^+(aq) + OH^-(aq) \\rightarrow NH_3(aq) + H_2O(l)}$$","","In terms of the equilibrium, the increase in $[\\mathrm{OH^-}]$ shifts it to the left, so almost all of the added base is removed and the pH stays close to 9.25.","","Because only a few drops were added, the amounts of $\\mathrm{NH_3}$ and $\\mathrm{NH_4^+}$ are far larger than the amount of acid or base added. The ratio $[\\mathrm{NH_3}]:[\\mathrm{NH_4^+}]$ changes only slightly, so the pH barely moves. Pure water would show a large change for the same additions."].join(`
