`),sampleAnswer:["**Step 1: Chloroethane (A) → Ethanol (B)**","","Reagents and conditions: Heat chloroethane with dilute aqueous potassium hydroxide ($\\mathrm{KOH(aq)}$) under reflux. The $\\mathrm{OH^-}$ ion undergoes nucleophilic substitution to replace the $\\mathrm{Cl}$ atom.","","Identification of ethanol (B): The mass spectrum will show a molecular ion peak at $m/z = 46$ (compared to $m/z = 64$ and $66$ for chloroethane due to the chlorine isotope pattern). The IR spectrum of ethanol will show a broad O–H absorption at $3230$–$3550\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, absent in chloroethane. Also, ethanol turns acidified potassium dichromate from orange to green (or decolourises acidified permanganate), while chloroethane does not react.","","**Step 2: Ethanol (B) → Ethanoic acid (C)**","","Reagents and conditions: Heat ethanol with excess acidified potassium permanganate ($\\mathrm{KMnO_4/H^+}$) or acidified potassium dichromate under reflux. The primary alcohol is fully oxidised to a carboxylic acid.","","Identification of ethanoic acid (C): The IR spectrum will show a carbonyl (C=O) peak at $1680$–$1750\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and a broad O–H carboxylic acid peak, absent in ethanol. The $^{13}\\mathrm{C}$ NMR will show a peak at $160$–$185\\ \\mathrm{ppm}$ (carboxyl carbon). Reactivity test: ethanoic acid produces $\\mathrm{CO_2}$ bubbles with sodium carbonate; ethanol does not.","","**Step 3: Ethanol (B) + Ethanoic acid (C) → Ethyl ethanoate (D)**","","Reagents and conditions: Heat a mixture of ethanol, ethanoic acid and concentrated sulfuric acid (catalyst) under reflux (Fischer esterification).","","Identification of ethyl ethanoate (D): The $^1\\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of ethyl ethanoate will have **3 signals**, whereas both ethanol and ethanoic acid have 2. The mass spectrum will show a molecular ion at $m/z = 88$ (ethanol: $46$; ethanoic acid: $60$). Ethyl ethanoate also has a characteristic sweet smell and will **not** produce $\\mathrm{CO_2}$ with sodium carbonate (no carboxylic acid)."].join(`
`),marks:7}}},EFe=[D9e,Y9e,V9e,Q9e,eMe,oMe,sMe,KRe,ZRe,nje,sje,cje,mje,gje,wje,Tje,Cje,Pje,Lje,Fje],OFe=[Dje,Yje,Xje,Zje,oFe,sFe,cFe,uFe,gFe,bFe,xFe,AFe,IFe,PFe],LFe=["CH-M8-2"],NFe=["Components of a mass spectrometer","Deflection of ions by $m/z$ ratio"],MFe={id:"chemistry/2020/q01",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...LFe],learningTopics:NFe,generate(t){const e=[1,2,3,0][t],n="It deflects the stream of charged particles.",o=["It detects the mass of the particles.","It excites electrons to higher energy levels.","It produces a stream of electrons that bombards the sample."],i=[];let a=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)i.push(s===e?n:o[a++]);return{question:"What is the function of the magnetic field in a mass spectrometer?",choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["The magnetic field bends the path of the charged particles (ions) as they travel through the mass spectrometer.","Because the amount of deflection depends on the mass-to-charge ratio, $m/z$, lighter ions or ions with a greater charge are deflected differently from heavier ions.","This is what allows the ions to be separated before they are detected. The magnetic field does not create electrons and it does not detect the ions itself; its role is to separate them by deflection."].join(`
