`),parts:[{question:"Give ONE hazard of working with these liquids and a precaution that reduces the risk.",sampleAnswer:["Hazard: all four liquids are **flammable**, and their vapours can ignite (heptane and ethanol are especially volatile).","","Precaution: keep the bottles well away from Bunsen burners, sparks and hot plates, use only small volumes and keep lids on. Working in a fume cupboard also stops vapour building up.","","A hazard must be matched with a precaution that actually reduces that hazard. Wearing gloves, for example, does nothing about flammability."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Identify the liquid in each bottle, giving reasons.",sampleAnswer:["| Bottle | Liquid |","| --- | --- |","| P | ethanol |","| Q | heptane |","| R | ethanoic acid |","| S | hept-1-ene |","","Use the two tests together:","- **Mixing with water.** Ethanol and ethanoic acid are small molecules with $\\mathrm{-OH}$ groups that hydrogen bond with water, so they mix completely. Heptane and hept-1-ene are non-polar hydrocarbons and form a separate layer.","- **Acidified permanganate.** A primary alcohol is oxidised (to ethanoic acid), and an alkene is oxidised at its $\\mathrm{C=C}$ double bond, so both decolourise the purple solution. An alkane and a carboxylic acid are not oxidised under these conditions.","","P mixes with water and is oxidised: **ethanol**. R mixes with water but is not oxidised: **ethanoic acid**. S is oxidised but does not mix: **hept-1-ene**. Q does neither: **heptane**."].join(`
