`),sampleAnswer:["**Molecular formula.** Alkanes have the general formula $\\mathrm{C_nH_{2n+2}}$, so","$$12.01n + 1.008(2n + 2) = 72.15 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 14.026n + 2.016 = 72.15 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; n = 5.00$$","The alkane is $\\mathrm{C_5H_{12}}$.","","**Isomers.** Draw the longest possible chain first, then shorten it by one carbon at a time and place the removed carbons as branches. Check each new structure is not just the same molecule drawn differently.","","**1. Pentane** (five-carbon chain):","$$\\mathrm{CH_3-CH_2-CH_2-CH_2-CH_3}$$","","**2. 2-methylbutane** (four-carbon chain with one methyl branch):","$$\\mathrm{CH_3-CH(CH_3)-CH_2-CH_3}$$",'Putting the methyl on carbon 3 gives the same molecule numbered from the other end, so "3-methylbutane" is not a separate isomer.',"","**3. 2,2-dimethylpropane** (three-carbon chain with two methyl branches on the middle carbon):","$$\\mathrm{CH_3-C(CH_3)_2-CH_3}$$","",'A methyl group on the end carbon of a chain simply extends the chain (for example, "1-methylbutane" is really pentane), so there are exactly **three** isomers of $\\mathrm{C_5H_{12}}$.'].join(`
