`),sampleAnswer:["All three compounds share the formula $\\mathrm{C_5H_{10}O}$, so every pair is a pair of isomers. The task is to decide what kind of isomerism links each pair.","","**Functional group isomers:** pentanal and pentan-2-one.","Both have an unbranched five-carbon chain, but pentanal has its $\\mathrm{C=O}$ at the end of the chain (an aldehyde, $\\mathrm{-CHO}$) while pentan-2-one has its $\\mathrm{C=O}$ between two carbons (a ketone). Same formula, different functional group.","","**Chain isomers:** pentanal and 3-methylbutanal.","Both are aldehydes, so the functional group is the same. The difference is the carbon skeleton: pentanal is a straight chain of five carbons, while 3-methylbutanal has a four-carbon main chain with a methyl branch.","","Pentan-2-one and 3-methylbutanal differ in both the functional group and the branching, so they are functional group isomers but not a clean example of chain isomers."].join(`
