`),marks:3},{question:"Using ideas about hydrogen bonding and dispersion forces, account for the trend within each series and for the difference between the two series.",sampleAnswer:["**Trend within each series.** In both series, boiling point rises with each extra carbon atom. Each additional $\\mathrm{CH_2}$ group adds 8 electrons and makes the molecule longer, which gives a larger, more easily distorted electron cloud and more surface contact between neighbouring molecules. The **dispersion forces** between molecules therefore get stronger, and more energy is needed to separate the molecules into a gas.","","**Difference between the series.** At every chain length the carboxylic acid boils far higher than the alkane (for example, methanoic acid boils at about 101°C but methane at about −162°C).","- Alkanes are non-polar. Dispersion forces are the only forces between their molecules.","- Carboxylic acids have a $\\mathrm{-COOH}$ group. The $\\mathrm{O-H}$ bond is highly polar, so acid molecules form **hydrogen bonds** to each other; pairs of acid molecules can even hold together with two hydrogen bonds. The $\\mathrm{C=O}$ group also adds dipole–dipole attractions.","- Hydrogen bonds are much stronger than the dispersion forces between small molecules, so much more energy is needed to boil the acids.","","An acid molecule also has more electrons than the alkane with the same number of carbon atoms, so its dispersion forces are a little stronger too. The main cause of the large gap, though, is hydrogen bonding."].join(`
