`),sampleAnswer:["The key is to compare how insoluble each possible precipitate is, using the $K_{sp}$ values on the data sheet:","","| Salt | $K_{sp}$ |","| --- | --- |","| $\\mathrm{BaCO_3}$ | $2.58 \\times 10^{-9}$ |","| $\\mathrm{CaCO_3}$ | $3.36 \\times 10^{-9}$ |","| $\\mathrm{CaSO_4}$ | $4.93 \\times 10^{-5}$ |","","The plan assumes that carbonate precipitates only barium. But calcium carbonate is almost as insoluble as barium carbonate.","","**Barium ions only — correct conclusion.**","In step 1, barium carbonate precipitates, so the student correctly concludes that barium is present:","$$\\mathrm{Ba^{2+}(aq) + CO_3^{2-}(aq) \\rightarrow BaCO_3(s)}$$","No calcium ions are present, so nothing precipitates in step 3 and the student correctly concludes that calcium is absent.","","**Barium and calcium ions — wrong conclusion.**","In step 1, the excess carbonate also precipitates the calcium ions as $\\mathrm{CaCO_3(s)}$. Both solids are removed together in step 2.","Because carbonate is in excess (say about $0.05\\ \\mathrm{mol\\ L^{-1}}$), the calcium left in the filtrate is tiny:","$$[\\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}] \\approx \\frac{3.36 \\times 10^{-9}}{0.05} \\approx 7 \\times 10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{mol\\ L^{-1}}$$","When sulfate is added in step 3, the ionic product $[\\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}][\\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}}]$ is about $10^{-9}$, far below $K_{sp}(\\mathrm{CaSO_4}) = 4.93 \\times 10^{-5}$. No precipitate forms, so the student wrongly concludes that calcium is absent.","","The plan fails because the reagent in step 1 is not selective. A better approach would test separate portions of the water, for example with a flame test (barium gives a pale green flame, calcium an orange-red flame)."].join(`
