`),sampleAnswer:["At the start of the reaction, the $^{13}\\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectrum shows **two signals**:","- One signal for the carbonyl carbon ($\\mathrm{C=O}$, approximately 200 ppm)","- One signal for the two equivalent $\\mathrm{CH_3}$ carbons (approximately 30 ppm)","","As the reaction proceeds, the $\\mathrm{C=O}$ bond is converted to $\\mathrm{C-OH}$.","A **new signal** appears for the central $\\mathrm{C-OH}$ carbon (approximately 65 ppm).","","As the reaction progresses:","- The original $\\mathrm{C=O}$ signal at ~200 ppm **decreases** in intensity","- The new $\\mathrm{C-OH}$ signal at ~65 ppm **increases** in intensity","","When the reaction is complete, the $\\mathrm{C=O}$ signal disappears entirely.","The relative intensities of the peaks can be used to determine the proportion of reactant remaining."].join(`
