`),choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["This is an example of convergence: a smartphone combines functions that once","needed several separate devices. People rarely leave home without their phone,","so using its mapping app means one less device to buy, carry and charge. That","convenience is the main reason for the change.","","Why the others are wrong:","- Dedicated GPS units are still made and sold, especially for remote and","  professional use, so they have not disappeared.",'- "Always more accurate" is false. Dedicated units often have stronger receivers',"  and work better in deep valleys or under thick tree cover.","- Phones usually run flat faster than dedicated GPS units, because the screen and","  other apps also use power, so battery life is not an advantage.","","The phone is adopted because it is convenient, not because it performs better."].join(`
`)}}}},Pit=["DT-DP-3","DT-DP-4"],Eit=["sustainable design and environmental impact","life cycle analysis, waste reduction, and product longevity","entrepreneurship and commercialisation of design ideas"],Oit={id:"design_and_technology/2019/q08",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...Pit],learningTopics:Eit,generate(t){const e=[3,0,1,2][t],n="Generating a need for individuals to keep updating existing products",o=["Constantly reviewing existing product lines","Incorporating technologies into the latest products","Encouraging people to change functioning products that they own"],i=[];let a=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)i.push(s===e?n:o[a++]);return{question:"Built-in obsolescence creates opportunities for ongoing profits for companies by",choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["Built-in obsolescence creates ongoing profits by generating a need for individuals to keep updating existing products.","","Built-in obsolescence is a deliberate design strategy where products are designed","to become outdated or less functional over time. This creates a continuous cycle","of consumer demand, as users feel compelled to purchase newer models to maintain","functionality or stay current — generating sustained revenue for companies.","","While options A and B describe product development activities, they do not","capture the core mechanism of built-in obsolescence. Option C is close but focuses",'on "functioning products" — built-in obsolescence specifically generates a perceived',"need to update, whether or not the existing product still works."].join(`
`)}}}},Lit=["DT-DP-2"],Nit=["Role of the end user in the design process","User-centred design","Identifying needs through consultation with users"],KF=[{product:"a new sports watch",correctPos:1,correct:"A running enthusiast",distractors:["A product designer","A business entrepreneur","A biomechanical engineer"],explanation:["A running enthusiast would most likely influence the features of a new sports watch.","","The end user — someone who regularly runs and relies on a sports watch — has direct","experience of what features are most useful, what is uncomfortable, and what is","missing from current products. Their practical knowledge makes them the most","influential voice in shaping the product's features.","","A product designer shapes the form and function but responds to user requirements.","A business entrepreneur focuses on profitability. A biomechanical engineer contributes","technical knowledge but is not the primary driver of feature selection."].join(`
`)},{product:"a new professional kitchen knife",correctPos:2,correct:"A professional chef",distractors:["A kitchenware retailer","A business entrepreneur","A materials engineer"],explanation:["A professional chef would most likely influence the features of a new professional kitchen knife.","","A professional chef uses knives extensively in their daily work and has detailed","knowledge of what balance, blade profile, handle grip, and edge retention are","needed for professional cooking. Their lived experience makes them the most","influential stakeholder when defining product features.","","A kitchenware retailer knows what sells but does not use a knife all day.","A business entrepreneur considers commercial viability. A materials engineer","contributes knowledge about blade materials but does not determine feature priorities."].join(`
`)},{product:"a new hospital patient monitoring device",correctPos:3,correct:"A registered nurse",distractors:["A product designer","A hospital administrator","A biomedical engineer"],explanation:["A registered nurse would most likely influence the features of a new hospital patient monitoring device.","","Registered nurses are the primary users of patient monitoring devices in clinical","settings. They have direct experience with the practical challenges of monitoring","patients — including alert systems, ease of reading data, hygiene requirements,","and workflow integration — making them the most influential stakeholders.","","A hospital administrator focuses on cost and procurement. A biomedical engineer","contributes technical expertise. A product designer gives form to requirements.","None of these have the daily hands-on experience of the nurse user."].join(`
