`),choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["Giving away an expensive product is a deliberate, long-term marketing strategy.","Students who spend years learning one animation program become fluent in its","tools and workflow. When they join or start studios, they tend to ask for the","software they know, and studios prefer software their new staff can already use.","The company gives up income now to win future paying customers and a larger","market share.","","Why the others are wrong:","- Education licences are not free to provide: servers, support and the lost","  sales are real costs, which is why this is a commercial risk.","- The students get a full, finished licence, not an unfinished test version.","- No law requires software companies to give products to students."].join(`
`)}}}},Hit=["DT-DP-12"],Bit=["Methods of evaluation in the design process","Prototype and user testing","Surveys and market feedback","Evaluating against criteria for success","Using evaluation to refine designs"],Dit={id:"design_and_technology/2019/q11",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Hit],learningTopics:Bit,generate(t){return{question:[],parts:[{question:"Identify TWO different methods of evaluation.",sampleAnswer:["Method 1: Prototype testing — constructing a physical or digital model of","the design and testing it against performance criteria.","","Method 2: Market survey — collecting structured feedback from the target","audience or potential users through questionnaires or interviews."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Describe how evaluation can aid the design of a product.",sampleAnswer:["Evaluation allows designers to test ideas and assess whether a design meets","its intended purpose before full-scale production begins.","","For example, a designer can create a prototype and test it against the","criteria to evaluate success established in the design brief. If the prototype","reveals problems — such as ergonomic discomfort or material weakness — the","designer can modify the design before costly manufacturing occurs.","","This iterative process of evaluation and refinement ensures the final product","better meets user needs, reduces waste, and improves overall design quality."].join(`
`),marks:3}]}}},zit=["DT-DP-2","DT-DP-8"],Wit=["CAD and CAM in the design process","Rapid prototyping and 3D printing","Simulation and virtual testing","Technology for communication and collaboration","Limitations of technology in design"],Yit={id:"design_and_technology/2019/q12",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...zit],learningTopics:Wit,generate(t){return{question:"Discuss the influence that technology can have on the design process.",sampleAnswer:["Technology, particularly CAD/CAM software, has significantly improved the design process","by enabling designers to create detailed three-dimensional digital models of their concepts.","Unlike traditional paper-based drawings, CAD models allow stakeholders to visualise and","evaluate a design in three dimensions before any physical production takes place, leading","to more informed decisions and fewer costly errors.","","Additionally, 3D printing technology allows designers to rapidly produce physical","prototypes directly from digital files. This accelerates the iteration cycle, as","designers can quickly test form, fit, and function, gather feedback, and refine","their design — all without the delays and expense of traditional prototyping methods.","","However, technology can also present challenges for the design process. Adopting new","software or equipment requires investment in training and hardware, which can increase","costs and slow down design workflows during the transition. Smaller design studios may","find it difficult to keep up with the pace of technological change.","","Overall, technology enhances the quality and efficiency of the design process but","requires careful management of associated costs and skill requirements."].join(`
`),marks:4}}},Uit=["DT-DP-14"],Git=["social trends and consumer influence on design","factors affecting product success, failure, and user acceptance"],Vit={id:"design_and_technology/2019/q13",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Uit],learningTopics:Git,generate(t){return{question:"Explain how changing social trends influence design practices.",sampleAnswer:["Changing social trends influence design practices because designers must respond to what","people value, how they live, and what problems they want solved. As trends change, the","brief, research, materials, features, and marketing of products also change.","","For example, increased environmental awareness has made sustainability a much stronger","design priority. Designers now research low-impact materials, reduce packaging, and","consider repair, reuse, and recycling much earlier in the design process because","consumers increasingly judge a product by its environmental impact.","","Another important trend is the growth of digital lifestyles. Because people work, shop,","and communicate online, designers use digital collaboration tools, gather feedback more","quickly, and often create products that integrate with apps, cloud systems, or smart","devices. This changes both what is designed and how the design team works.","","Demographic trends also matter. An ageing population, for instance, encourages designers","to apply universal design principles such as clearer displays, easier controls, and safer","product interaction. In this way, social change directly influences design decisions.","","Therefore, changing social trends influence design practices by shifting consumer","expectations and forcing designers to adapt their methods and solutions to remain relevant."].join(`
`),marks:6}}},Xit=["DT-IET-1","DT-DP-14"],Kit=["innovation and emerging technologies in design","social, ethical, and environmental impacts of innovation","social trends and consumer influence on design","factors affecting product success, failure, and user acceptance"],Qit={id:"design_and_technology/2019/q14",version:1,variants:1,syllabusTopics:[...Xit],learningTopics:Kit,generate(t){return{question:"Analyse factors that could determine the success or failure of an innovative design.",sampleAnswer:["The success or failure of an innovative design is shaped by a complex interplay","of factors spanning timing, social context, technology, marketing, function,","and cultural considerations. Understanding these factors is critical for designers","and companies seeking to bring new products to market.","","**Timing**","","The timing of a product launch can be decisive. Releasing a product aligned with","seasonal demand or a cultural moment increases the likelihood of consumer uptake.","Conversely, launching a product too early — before supporting infrastructure exists —","can condemn it to failure. The early electric vehicle market illustrates this: high","battery costs and limited charging infrastructure initially restricted consumer","adoption, despite the innovation being technologically sound.","","**Social Trends**","","Products that align with prevailing social values tend to succeed. The rise of","meal-kit delivery services (providing pre-portioned ingredients and recipes delivered","to the home) succeeded because they addressed the social trend towards convenience","and time-poor lifestyles. Designers who monitor and respond to shifting social","values gain a significant competitive advantage.","","**Function and Available Technology**","","An innovative design must work as intended. If the underlying technology is not","sufficiently mature, the product will fail to meet user expectations. For example,","early Bluetooth headphones suffered from poor audio quality and unreliable connections,","limiting their success until the technology improved. Function is a foundational","requirement: no amount of marketing can sustain a product that consistently fails in use.","","**Marketing Strategies**","","Even a well-designed product can fail if its value proposition is poorly communicated.","Marketing must accurately represent what the product does and who it is for.","Misleading or ineffective marketing can erode consumer trust and damage brand","reputation. The recall of certain automotive safety systems that caused injuries","upon deployment demonstrates how failures in delivering on marketed promises","can have severe commercial and safety consequences.","","**Cultural Factors**","","A design that ignores the cultural context of its intended users risks failure.","Introducing advanced technologies into communities without reliable electricity","or digital literacy support, for example, will fail regardless of the innovation's","technical merit. Successful designers research the cultural norms, infrastructure,","and values of their target market and adapt their designs accordingly.","","**Aesthetics**","","Visual appeal and product aesthetics contribute significantly to consumer decision-making.","In competitive markets such as consumer electronics, a product that is technically","capable but visually unappealing will lose market share to more attractive alternatives.","Emerging display technologies — such as wrap-around screens — show how aesthetic","innovation can differentiate products and drive adoption.","","**Conclusion**","","The success of an innovative design depends not on any single factor but on how well","the design integrates functional excellence with cultural relevance, appropriate timing,","strong marketing, and aesthetic appeal. Designers who analyse these factors holistically","are far better positioned to create innovations that endure in the marketplace."].join(`
`),marks:15}}},Jit=[lit,dit,git,bit,Tit,Cit,qit,Oit,Mit,Fit],Zit=[Dit,Yit,Vit,Qit],eat=["DT-DP-3"],tat=["design factors and criteria for success","selecting materials and features to suit purpose","ergonomics, anthropometrics, and inclusive design","designing for user comfort, safety, and accessibility"],nat={id:"design_and_technology/2020/q01",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...eat],learningTopics:tat,generate(t){const e=[3,0,1,2][t],n="The study of how people work efficiently within their physical environment",o=["The study of human body measurement","The design of products to satisfy a niche market","Design factors to consider in developing energy-efficient products"],i=[];let a=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)i.push(s===e?n:o[a++]);return{question:"Which of the following best describes ergonomics?",choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["Ergonomics is the scientific study of how people interact with their working environment,","focusing on designing systems, tools, and products so people can use them comfortably,","safely, and efficiently.","","Option A describes anthropometrics (the study of human body measurements), not ergonomics.","Options B and C describe aspects of product design and sustainability, which are related","but are not definitions of ergonomics."].join(`
