`)}}}},sst=["DT-DP-3","DT-DP-4"],rst=["Economic factors affecting design and production","Impact of design on communities and society","Responding to social change through design","New technologies reducing production costs","Planned obsolescence and its economic effects"],lst={id:"design_and_technology/2021/q05",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...sst],learningTopics:rst,generate(t){const e=[0,1,2,3][t],n="Adapting the design to the town's changing needs and using newer production technology to keep the price low",o=["Choosing materials mainly because they have a small environmental footprint","Comparing design options mainly to maximise the profit on each filter sold","Giving the filter a deliberately short working life so it is cheaper to make"],i=[];let a=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)i.push(s===e?n:o[a++]);return{question:["A designer is developing a household water filter for a regional town where many","families have lost income after a local factory closed.","","Which action best shows the designer taking into account the economic impact of","the product on this community?"].join(`
`),choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["The focus is the economic effect on the community, so the key question is whether","families can afford and benefit from the product. The town has changed: incomes","have fallen. A responsible designer responds to that social change and uses newer","technology, such as more efficient moulding or cheaper filter media, to lower the","cost, so the filter is affordable for the people who need it.","","Choosing low-footprint materials is an environmental decision, not an economic one","for the community. Maximising profit per filter benefits the business and could","make the product harder for families to afford. A deliberately short working life","(planned obsolescence) lowers the cost of making each unit, but families must keep","buying replacements, so it costs the community more over time."].join(`
`)}}}},cst=["DT-DP-4"],hst=["Designing for societal needs and values","Principles of sustainable product design","Quality and durability extending product life","Distinguishing product-related from management-related factors","Consumer needs and market acceptance of products"],dst={id:"design_and_technology/2021/q06",version:1,variants:4,syllabusTopics:[...cst],learningTopics:hst,generate(t){const e=[2,3,0,1][t],n="Making well-built backpacks that match what students and families really need, so they stay in use for years",o=["Launching the range just before the school year starts to boost sales","Recording how much fabric is used without changing the design","Keeping the design team on schedule with regular planning meetings"],i=[];let a=0;for(let s=0;s<4;s++)i.push(s===e?n:o[a++]);return{question:["A company wants its new range of school backpacks to be efficient and sustainable.","","Which approach would contribute most to this goal?"].join(`
`),choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["A product is sustainable when the resources used to make it deliver as much value","as possible over a long life. A backpack that is well made and matches what users","need, such as the right size, comfortable straps and strong zips, will be used for","several years instead of being thrown away and replaced. That makes efficient use of","materials and cuts waste, so this approach contributes most.","","Timing the launch is a marketing decision and does not change the product.","Recording fabric use is a first step, but it does not reduce resource use unless","the design changes. Regular planning meetings help manage the project, but they do","not make the backpack itself more efficient or sustainable."].join(`
`)}}}},ust=["DT-DP-14"],mst=["client needs analysis and target market research","user-centred design decision-making","social trends and consumer influence on design","factors affecting product success, failure, and user acceptance"],pst="For consumers, what is the most direct advantage of this change?",fst="Greater convenience",tH=[{context:["Supermarkets are replacing staffed checkouts with self-service kiosks and","phone apps that let shoppers scan items as they go and pay without queuing."].join(`
`),correctPos:2,distractors:["Higher profits for supermarkets","Better protection of personal data","Less packaging waste"],explanation:["**Greater convenience** is the most direct advantage for shoppers. They can scan","and pay at their own pace, skip the checkout queue and finish shopping faster.","","Higher profits (from employing fewer checkout staff) go to the supermarket, not","to the shopper.","","Personal data is not better protected: shopping apps usually collect more","information about what and when people buy than a cash purchase would.","","The same products and packaging are sold whichever way the customer pays, so","packaging waste is not reduced by this change."].join(`
`)},{context:["Video streaming services have grown significantly, replacing physical media","such as DVDs and CDs. Consumers can now access thousands of films, TV shows,","and albums instantly on demand."].join(`
`),correctPos:0,distractors:["Increased revenue for retailers","Greater ownership of content","Reduced screen time"],explanation:["**Greater convenience** is the most direct advantage to consumers from the rise","of video streaming services. Consumers can instantly access a vast library of","content from any device at any time, without needing to purchase, store, or","return physical media.","","Increased revenue goes to retailers and streaming companies, not to","individual consumers.","","Greater ownership of content is the opposite of what streaming provides:","consumers usually licence content rather than own it, so they own less than","when they bought physical media.","","Reduced screen time is not a consequence of streaming growth; on-demand","content tends to increase screen time."].join(`
`)},{context:["Online banking and digital financial services have expanded rapidly, allowing","consumers to manage accounts, transfer funds, and pay bills without visiting","a bank branch."].join(`
`),correctPos:1,distractors:["Increased employment in banking","Greater bank profits","Increased privacy protections"],explanation:["**Greater convenience** is the most direct advantage to consumers from the","expansion of online banking. Consumers can access their accounts, transfer","money, and pay bills at any time from anywhere, removing the need to travel","to a branch during business hours.","","Increased employment in banking is not a consumer benefit, and digital banking","has often reduced the need for branch staff.","","Greater bank profits benefit financial institutions, not consumers.","","Increased privacy protections are not a guaranteed outcome. Digital services","introduce cybersecurity risks, and consumers may be more exposed to data","breaches than with in-person banking."].join(`
`)},{context:["Food-delivery apps now let people browse menus from hundreds of local","restaurants, order with a few taps and track the rider to their door."].join(`
`),correctPos:3,distractors:["Higher earnings for restaurants","More control over personal location data","Healthier eating habits"],explanation:["**Greater convenience** is the most direct advantage for the people ordering.","They can choose from many restaurants and have a meal brought to them without","travelling, queuing or phoning in an order.","","Higher earnings go to restaurants (and the app’s commission often cuts their","margins anyway), so this is not a consumer benefit.","","Delivery apps track the customer’s address and location, so users have less","control over location data, not more.","","Easy access to takeaway food tends to encourage less healthy eating rather than","healthier habits."].join(`
