`),choices:i,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["Forms of economic integration can be ranked by how much policy the members share. The clue in this treaty is that the nations give up their own currencies and hand interest-rate decisions to one central bank.","","Sharing a currency and a monetary policy is the defining feature of a monetary union. The euro area, where members use the euro and the European Central Bank sets one cash rate, is the best-known example.","","A free trade area only removes trade barriers between the members, and each member keeps its own currency and its own tariffs on outsiders. A customs union goes one step further by adding a common external tariff, but members still keep separate currencies. A preferential trade agreement is the weakest form: it only lowers some barriers on selected goods. None of these three involves a common currency, so they do not fit the treaty."].join(`
