`),choices:n,sampleAnswer:{correctChoiceIndex:e,explanation:["The key idea is how a star cluster forms. All of its members condense out","of one giant cloud of gas and dust within a short period compared with a","star's lifetime, so astronomers treat them as having been born together.","The shared property is therefore their **age**.","","The diagram itself shows that the other options vary from star to star:","","- The points spread from a colour index of about $0.5$ to about $1.2$, so the","  stars are not all the same **colour**.","- The visual magnitudes range from about $12$ to about $20$. Every star is at","  nearly the same distance from Earth, so this spread is a real spread in","  **luminosity**.","- Stars further up the main sequence are more massive, so the **masses**","  also differ.","","The shape of the plot supports the common age. The upper main sequence is","missing because the massive, short-lived stars have already left it. The","turn-off point where the main sequence bends towards the giant branch acts","as a clock for the cluster's age. That only works because the stars formed","at the same time."].join(`
