`),sampleAnswer:["**Bohr model:**","","The electron is a negatively charged **particle** travelling in fixed circular","orbits around the nucleus. Only certain orbits are allowed — those with","quantised angular momentum ($L = n\\hbar$). Electrons do not radiate energy","while in a stable orbit, but emit or absorb a photon of specific energy","when transitioning between orbits.","","**de Broglie model:**","","de Broglie proposed that the electron has **wave–particle duality**: it has a","wavelength $\\lambda = h/mv$. For a stable orbit, the electron must form a","**standing wave** — the circumference of the orbit must be an integer multiple","of the electron's wavelength:","","$$2\\pi r = n\\lambda$$","","This gave a physical justification for Bohr's quantisation condition.","","**Schrödinger model:**","","Schrödinger described the electron using a **wave equation** whose solution","$\\psi$ (the wavefunction) gives the probability amplitude for finding the","electron at a given location. The electron has no definite orbit; instead,","its position is described by a **probability distribution** (the electron 'cloud').","This model correctly predicts atomic spectra and chemical bonding."].join(`
`),marks:5}}const LGn=1,NGn={id:"physics/2021/q29",version:1,variants:LGn,syllabusTopics:[...PGn],learningTopics:EGn,generate:OGn},MGn=["PH-M6-1"],RGn=["charged particle acceleration in electric fields","kinetic energy and distance","electric work"];function jGn(t){return{question:["A proton starts from rest at the centre of a parallel-plate capacitor.","The plates are separated by $12$ cm, so the proton starts $6.0$ cm from each plate.","An electric field accelerates the proton toward the negative plate."].join(`
