`),marks:3}]}}const aJn=1,sJn={id:"physics/2022/q30",version:1,variants:aJn,syllabusTopics:[...nJn],learningTopics:oJn,generate:iJn},rJn=["PH-M8-2"],lJn=["Bohr model","atomic structure","quantized energy levels"];function cJn(t){return{question:["Evaluate how the Bohr–Rutherford model of the atom accounts for:","- the results of the Geiger–Marsden experiment, and","- the hydrogen emission spectrum.","","In your answer, include a labelled diagram, a relevant calculation,","and references to energy, forces, and photons."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["## Geiger–Marsden Experiment","","Geiger and Marsden fired alpha particles at a thin gold foil. They observed:","- Most alpha particles passed straight through.","- A small fraction were deflected at large angles.","- A very small number (about 1 in 8000) bounced almost straight back.","","The **Rutherford nuclear model** explains these results:","- The atom is mostly **empty space**, so most alphas pass through undeflected.","- A tiny, dense, positively charged **nucleus** at the centre contains almost","  all the mass.","- The rare large-angle deflections occur when an alpha particle comes very","  close to the nucleus; the strong **electrostatic (Coulomb) repulsion** between","  the positive nucleus and positive alpha particle deflects it sharply.","","*(Diagram: atom with small nucleus at centre; arrows showing most alphas","passing through, a few slightly deflected, one bouncing back.)*","","## Hydrogen Emission Spectrum","","Bohr extended Rutherford's model by proposing that electrons orbit the nucleus","in **fixed circular orbits** (stationary states) with specific quantised energies.","","**Forces:** The electrostatic (Coulomb) attraction between the negative electron","and positive nucleus provides the centripetal force for circular orbit:","$$F = \\frac{ke^2}{r^2} = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$$","","**Quantised energy levels:** For hydrogen, the energy of level $n$ is:","$$E_n = -\\frac{13.6}{n^2} \\ \\text{eV}$$","","Electrons can only exist in these allowed levels; they cannot spiral inward","(addressing the classical instability problem).","","**Photon emission:** When an electron transitions from a higher energy level","$n_i$ to a lower level $n_f$, it emits a **photon** with energy equal to","the difference:","$$E_{\\text{photon}} = hf = E_{n_i} - E_{n_f}$$","","**Example calculation** (Balmer series, $n = 3 \\to n = 2$):","$$E_{\\text{photon}} = \\left(-\\frac{13.6}{9}\\right) - \\left(-\\frac{13.6}{4}\\right)","= -1.51 - (-3.40) = 1.89 \\ \\text{eV}$$","","$$f = \\frac{E_{\\text{photon}}}{h} = \\frac{1.89 \\times 1.602 \\times 10^{-19}}","{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}} \\approx 4.57 \\times 10^{14} \\ \\text{Hz}$$","","This corresponds to red light ($\\lambda \\approx 656$ nm), matching the observed","$\\mathrm{H}\\alpha$ line in the hydrogen spectrum.","","Since only specific energy differences are allowed, only specific frequencies","of light are emitted, producing the **discrete line spectrum** of hydrogen.","","## Limitations","","While the Bohr–Rutherford model successfully explains the hydrogen spectrum","and Geiger–Marsden results, it cannot explain multi-electron atoms, the","relative intensities of spectral lines, or fine structure — these require","quantum mechanics."].join(`
`),marks:9}}const hJn=1,dJn={id:"physics/2022/q31",version:1,variants:hJn,syllabusTopics:[...rJn],learningTopics:lJn,generate:cJn},uJn=["PH-M6-3"],mJn=["electromagnetic induction","eddy currents","Lenz's law"];function pJn(t){return{question:["An exercise bike has permanent magnets positioned near an aluminium flywheel.","When the flywheel spins, the rider experiences resistance (braking torque)."].join(`
`),parts:[{question:["Explain, using the concepts of electromagnetic induction and Lenz's law,","how the magnets make it harder to pedal."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**How magnets cause resistance:**","","1. As the aluminium flywheel spins, different parts of the flywheel move","   through the magnetic field of the permanent magnets.","","2. By Faraday's law, the changing magnetic flux through the conducting","   aluminium induces **eddy currents** (circulating currents) in the flywheel.","","3. By **Lenz's law**, these induced currents flow in a direction that opposes","   the change that caused them — i.e. they create a magnetic force that","   **opposes the rotation** of the flywheel.","","4. This opposing force acts as a **braking torque**, requiring the rider to","   exert more effort (force on pedals) to maintain speed."].join(`
`),marks:3},{question:["The magnets can be moved closer to the flywheel to increase resistance.","Justify why moving the magnets closer increases the braking torque."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Why moving magnets closer increases braking torque:**","","- Moving the magnets closer to the flywheel increases the **magnetic field","  strength** $B$ in the aluminium at that location.","","- A stronger field means a **greater rate of change of flux** as the flywheel","  rotates, inducing **larger eddy currents** (by Faraday's law: $\\varepsilon \\propto \\Delta\\Phi/\\Delta t$).","","- Larger eddy currents experience a greater force ($F = BIL$) opposing motion,","  producing a **greater braking torque** and making it harder to pedal."].join(`
`),marks:3}]}}const fJn=1,gJn={id:"physics/2022/q32",version:1,variants:fJn,syllabusTopics:[...uJn],learningTopics:mJn,generate:pJn},$Jn=["PH-M5-2","PH-M5-1"],yJn=["circular motion","centripetal acceleration","projectile range"];function bJn(t){return{question:["An athlete throws a hammer in a circular path.","The hammer has mass $m = 7.0$ kg, radius of circular motion $r = 1.6$ m,","and period of revolution $T = 0.50$ s.","","The hammer is released at point P, which is $1.2$ m above the ground.","At release, the velocity is directed at $45°$ above the horizontal."].join(`
`),parts:[{question:["Show that the vertical component of velocity at P is approximately","$14.2\\ \\mathrm{m\\ s^{-1}}$."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Vertical component of velocity:**","","The speed of the hammer in its circular path:","$$v = \\frac{2\\pi r}{T} = \\frac{2\\pi \\times 1.6}{0.50} = \\frac{10.053}{0.50} = 20.1 \\ \\text{m s}^{-1}$$","","The vertical component at 45°:","$$v_y = v \\sin 45° = 20.1 \\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = 20.1 \\times 0.7071 \\approx 14.2 \\ \\text{m s}^{-1} \\checkmark$$"].join(`
