`),sampleAnswer:["**Particles X and Y — positive charges, curving upward:**","","The magnetic force on a moving charge is given by $\\mathbf{F} = q\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{B}$.","Since both X and Y curve in the same direction (upward), they must carry the","**same sign of charge**. Given the direction of curvature (upward, with $\\mathbf{B}$","into the page and velocity to the right), the right-hand rule gives an upward","force for **positive charges**. So X and Y are positively charged.","","**Particle Z — negative charge, curving downward:**","","Particle Z curves in the **opposite direction** to X and Y.","A negative charge experiences a force opposite to that for a positive charge","under the same conditions. Therefore, Z is **negatively charged**.","","**Radius of curvature:**","","For a charged particle moving in a magnetic field, the radius is:","$$r = \\frac{mv}{qB}$$","","Since all particles enter with the same speed $v$, and $B$ is constant:","","$$r \\propto \\frac{m}{q}$$","","Particle X has a **smaller radius** than particle Y, so X has a **smaller","charge-to-mass ratio $m/q$** (or larger $q/m$).","","This means either:","- X has a smaller mass than Y (same charge), or","- X has a larger charge than Y (same mass), or","- a combination of both.","","The tighter curvature of X compared with Y means that the magnetic force","provides a centripetal acceleration over a shorter radius, consistent with","a smaller $m/q$ ratio for X."].join(`
