`),parts:[{question:"Analyse qualitatively the energy changes as the satellite moves from P to Q.",sampleAnswer:["As the satellite moves from P to Q, it gains altitude (increases orbital radius).","Its kinetic energy decreases and its gravitational potential energy increases,","consistent with the conservation of energy. The total mechanical energy remains","constant throughout the trajectory (no engines are firing)."].join(`
`),marks:2},{question:"Show that the kinetic energy of the satellite at Q is $1.194 \\times 10^{10}$ J.",sampleAnswer:["Immediately after the engine fires at P, the total energy of the satellite is:","","$$E_\\text{new} = E_P + \\Delta KE = -1.195 \\times 10^{10} + 5.232 \\times 10^8 = -1.1427 \\times 10^{10} \\text{ J}$$","","The gravitational potential energy at Q is:","","$$U_Q = -\\frac{GMm}{r_Q} = -\\frac{6.67 \\times 10^{-11} \\times 6.0 \\times 10^{24} \\times 400}{6.850 \\times 10^6}","= -2.337 \\times 10^{10} \\text{ J}$$","","By conservation of energy:","","$$KE_Q = E_\\text{new} - U_Q = -1.1427 \\times 10^{10} - (-2.337 \\times 10^{10})$$","","$$= 1.194 \\times 10^{10} \\text{ J} \\checkmark$$"].join(`
`),marks:4},{question:"Explain the motion of the satellite after it passes through Q.",sampleAnswer:["At Q, the satellite's velocity exceeds the circular orbital velocity for that radius","(since its kinetic energy is greater than that required for a circular orbit at $r_Q$).","Therefore the satellite continues to climb, trading kinetic energy for potential energy.","It follows an elliptical orbit with Q as the perigee (closest point) of the new","elliptical path. The satellite oscillates between this closest point and a furthest","point (apogee) while conserving total mechanical energy."].join(`
