`),marks:3},{question:"Explain how the dark lines in this spectrum are produced.",sampleAnswer:["**Continuous source.** The star's hot, dense surface emits a **continuous**","spectrum, with light of every wavelength (black-body radiation).","","**Selective absorption.** The light then passes through the cooler, less dense","hydrogen of the atmosphere. Electrons in hydrogen atoms can only have certain","energies. A photon is absorbed only if its energy $E = hf$ exactly equals the","difference between two energy levels. The electron then jumps to the higher","level. Photons of all other energies pass through.","","**Re-emission in all directions.** The excited atoms soon return to lower","levels and emit photons of the same energies, but in **random directions**.","So much less light of these particular wavelengths continues towards the","observer.","","**Result.** Those wavelengths appear as **dark lines** on the bright continuous","background. Their positions identify hydrogen's energy levels (here the","Balmer series, with transitions from $n = 2$)."].join(`
