`),wrongExplanations:[["A circular orbit around the planet","A circular orbit requires $E < 0$ and a specific horizontal speed $v_c = v/\\sqrt{2}$ at the surface."],["An elliptical orbit around the planet","An elliptical orbit requires $E < 0$, i.e. launch speed less than $v$."],["A parabolic trajectory, returning to land with velocity $v$","A body with $E = 0$ escapes to infinity and never returns."]]},{launchDesc:"at $\\dfrac{v}{2}$",correctChoice:"A path that rises to a maximum height and then falls back to the surface",correctPos:2,correctExplanation:["The launch speed $v/2$ is **less** than the escape velocity $v$.","","Total mechanical energy:","","$$E = \\frac{1}{2}m\\left(\\frac{v}{2}\\right)^2 - \\frac{GMm}{R}","= \\frac{mv^2}{8} - \\frac{mv^2}{2} = -\\frac{3mv^2}{8} < 0$$","","(using $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = \\frac{GMm}{R}$ from the definition of $v$)","","Negative total energy means the mass is **bound** to the planet: it cannot","escape, so it slows down, reaches a maximum height and falls back.","","It also cannot go into orbit. With no atmosphere and no further thrust, the","path of the mass always passes back through its launch point **on the surface**,","so any path it follows around the planet runs into the ground. It lands again","with speed $v/2$ (by conservation of energy)."].join(`
