`),sampleAnswer:["The two types of radiation differ in how far they penetrate matter and in","the sign of their charge. A good answer gives a method for each test and","says what result identifies each source.","","**Test 1: absorption by paper**","","Clamp a Geiger–Müller tube a few centimetres from one source and record","the count rate. Then place a sheet of paper between the source and the","tube and record the count rate again. Repeat for the other source, and","measure the background count with no source present.","","- Alpha particles are very strongly ionising and are stopped by the paper,","  so the count drops to **background**: this is the alpha source.","- Beta particles pass through paper (they need a few millimetres of","  aluminium to stop them), so the count stays **well above background**:","  this is the beta source.","","**Test 2: deflection in a magnetic field**","","Pass a narrow beam from each source through a uniform magnetic field at","right angles to the beam, and use the detector to find which way the beam","bends. (This works best in a vacuum, because alpha particles only travel","a few centimetres in air.)","","- Alpha particles are positive ($+2e$) and beta-minus particles are","  negative ($-e$), so the two beams bend in **opposite directions**.","- The source whose beam bends the way the right-hand rule predicts for a","  positive charge is the alpha source.","- The beta beam is also bent much more sharply, because a beta particle","  has a far smaller mass.","","Other acceptable tests include measuring the range in air (alpha counts","fall to background within about 5 cm) or deflection by an electric field."].join(`
