`),sampleAnswer:["**Difference 1 — Probability clouds, not fixed orbits**","","In the Bohr (and Rutherford) model, electrons travel in definite circular","orbits at fixed radii; their position and momentum at any instant are","precisely specified.","","In Schrödinger's model, the electron is described by a wave function $\\psi$.","The square of the wave function, $|\\psi|^2$, gives only the **probability**","of finding the electron in a given region of space. There is no defined","path or orbit — only a probability distribution (electron cloud).","","**Difference 2 — Wave nature of the electron**","","The Rutherford and Bohr models treat electrons as classical point particles","moving along well-defined trajectories.","","Schrödinger's model treats electrons as **matter waves**, described by a","wave function consistent with de Broglie's hypothesis. The three-dimensional","shapes of orbitals (s, p, d, …) arise directly from the wave-like solutions","to Schrödinger's equation, and phenomena such as electron diffraction are","naturally explained by this wave nature."].join(`
